The first attempt used `../../` and landed on `FILEERROR=1` because that reached past `/home` entirely, into territory `archivist` cannot write to. One directory level too enthusiastic. Dialing it back to a single `../` (since `_root` is already `/home/archivist/printer`) put it exactly where it needed to be.

```bash
ssh -i /tmp/archivist_key archivist@127.0.0.1
```

```text
archivist@paperwork:~$
```

> **📝 My Explanation:**

The printer's "filesystem sandbox" had exactly one `../` worth of structural integrity, and not a single dot more. I dropped my own SSH public key into `archivist`'s `authorized_keys` through a fake printer's file upload feature, then simply SSH'd in like I was invited. No exploit development, no fuzzing, just very polite path traversal and one embarrassing miscount of relative directories.

### 💭 Side Thought

Between the LPD command injection and this PJL traversal, this box has now let me become two completely different users purely by talking nicely to printers. I genuinely miss the days when popping a shell required more than being polite to a print spooler.

